Subject: [PATCH 4/8] WP-0.7, enforcement (cld-advisor's series 4, patch 4;
 built on 323f92327 with series 2, series 3 and the first three patches of
 series 4 applied): A TEST OF A LOCK IS NOT SWITCHED OFF WITHOUT A RECORDED
 ACT (main's unseen case for series 2 that was caught by accident, M5). A test
 made to skip was caught only because its file's hash is listed; no gate
 looked for a skip, and a test outside the lists could be switched off unseen.
 THE MEND is the rule of series 2, taken down from the lock file to the test:
 since the anchor, a test function does not leave its lock, and does not gain
 a way not to run, without a recorded act. What can keep a test from running
 is read from the lock's source and not by running it: a skip, skipif or xfail
 mark on the test, on its class or on its module; a call in its body that
 skips; a skip at the module's import; a module told that it holds no test. A
 test that left is named under 'tests' in LEFT_BY_NAME; one that may now skip,
 under 'skips' in RAISED_BY_NAME. A new test may come with a skip, as main's
 slow lanes do; a lock added after the anchor is held to what it was when it
 was added; a skip may be taken off freely. On main nothing is named: since
 the anchor no test has left a lock and none has gained a skip; in the last
 thousand commits five locks lost eight tests and four tests gained a skip,
 about one such act in a hundred commits. What it does not reach: a skip that
 a helper or a fixture decides; a test whose body is rewritten to assert
 something else; a test that runs only where a variable is set and nobody sets
 it. The lock: six tests added to tests/test_nothing_leaves_a_ledger.py, eight
 forms of a switch; on the commit before, four fail because the gate passes.
 Tested against planted errors, 11 of 11 forms; computed once. 0 of 19.

